WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.610 --> 00:00:04.630
Welcome to Ear Biscuits,

00:00:04.630 --> 00:00:06.260
the podcast where two lifelong friends

00:00:06.260 --> 00:00:07.870
talk about life for a long time.

00:00:07.870 --> 00:00:08.703
I'm Rhett.

00:00:08.703 --> 00:00:10.000
And I'm Link.

00:00:10.000 --> 00:00:13.640
This week at the round
table of dim lighting,

00:00:13.640 --> 00:00:18.100
we are talking about ch-ch-ch-changes.

00:00:18.100 --> 00:00:19.820
Yeah, we asked you ...
Changing your mind.

00:00:19.820 --> 00:00:23.430
We asked you, where we
ask these questions,

00:00:23.430 --> 00:00:26.790
which is Twitter, on the Mythical account,

00:00:26.790 --> 00:00:28.363
to tell us a time that you

00:00:28.363 --> 00:00:30.320
changed your perspective on something.

00:00:30.320 --> 00:00:31.910
Could be small like a food preference,

00:00:31.910 --> 00:00:35.930
could be something big like
a major belief and why.

00:00:35.930 --> 00:00:38.163
And we got a lot of responses.

00:00:38.163 --> 00:00:39.451
Yeah, I'm excited about
talking about this.

00:00:39.451 --> 00:00:43.780
Yank in your laptop here so
we can look at some responses,

00:00:43.780 --> 00:00:47.223
but, you know we wanted to
talk about this because,

00:00:49.240 --> 00:00:51.490
and kind of mission accomplished

00:00:51.490 --> 00:00:53.970
as far as I'm concerned here,

00:00:53.970 --> 00:00:55.200
because we were hoping

00:00:55.200 --> 00:00:59.230
that it would just be
a refreshing sensation

00:00:59.230 --> 00:01:03.590
of people sharing how
they changed their mind.

00:01:03.590 --> 00:01:07.777
And I don't know, there's a
humility associated with saying,

00:01:07.777 --> 00:01:09.830
"I once thought this."

00:01:09.830 --> 00:01:11.194
And even if it's a minor preference,

00:01:11.194 --> 00:01:13.417
"But now I think this,

00:01:13.417 --> 00:01:16.897
"I disagree with my former
self about this thing,

00:01:16.897 --> 00:01:18.390
"big or small."

00:01:18.390 --> 00:01:21.008
It's something to celebrate
in this day and age

00:01:21.008 --> 00:01:25.950
when people just dig in and
there's no conversations.

00:01:25.950 --> 00:01:29.619
And not that this conversation
is gonna get political,

00:01:29.619 --> 00:01:31.020
necessarily.

00:01:31.020 --> 00:01:33.260
I mean, we got some lighthearted stuff and

00:01:33.260 --> 00:01:35.890
some heavier stuff, but it
really doesn't get into,

00:01:35.890 --> 00:01:38.920
'cause we, you know, we
talk about those things

00:01:38.920 --> 00:01:40.230
in other, well, there's one thing

00:01:40.230 --> 00:01:41.350
that kind of gets a
little political in this,

00:01:41.350 --> 00:01:43.750
but anyway, one of the reasons

00:01:43.750 --> 00:01:45.530
I was so excited about talking about this

00:01:45.530 --> 00:01:47.882
is all the things that you just said.

00:01:47.882 --> 00:01:49.870
But also I think that, you know,

00:01:49.870 --> 00:01:52.840
there's a little bit of a
selfish, a self-interest in this,

00:01:52.840 --> 00:01:55.050
which is like, you know,

00:01:55.050 --> 00:01:57.300
in ways that we have
shared on this podcast

00:01:57.300 --> 00:01:58.450
we've changed our minds.

00:01:58.450 --> 00:02:00.420
And I'm kind of fascinated

00:02:00.420 --> 00:02:03.690
with the reasons why
people change their mind.

00:02:03.690 --> 00:02:04.630
Because I think a lot of times

00:02:04.630 --> 00:02:07.610
it's not the reason that
you state, you know?

00:02:07.610 --> 00:02:09.220
And there's just all kinds of factors.

00:02:09.220 --> 00:02:14.130
And I think we got some really
honest, honest responses

00:02:14.130 --> 00:02:16.920
and I'm just fascinated
with the concept of

00:02:16.920 --> 00:02:20.520
perspective change and what leads to it.

00:02:20.520 --> 00:02:22.810
And like you said, I just
think it's so timely,

00:02:22.810 --> 00:02:27.650
because it seems that
there's so many people

00:02:27.650 --> 00:02:30.400
spending more of their energy

00:02:30.400 --> 00:02:33.520
attempting to change other people's minds

00:02:33.520 --> 00:02:37.060
in ways that we haven't really seen before

00:02:37.060 --> 00:02:38.471
in like discourse, right?

00:02:38.471 --> 00:02:40.370
I was talking to Jesse the other day

00:02:40.370 --> 00:02:42.390
and I was like, you know,

00:02:42.390 --> 00:02:44.540
she hates going on Facebook,

00:02:44.540 --> 00:02:48.370
because Facebook has
become a place where you

00:02:49.370 --> 00:02:53.430
kinda just say, "This is who
I am, this is what I believe."

00:02:53.430 --> 00:02:55.620
And then argue with people
who differ with you.

00:02:55.620 --> 00:02:57.737
You know, I think at
first it was kind of like,

00:02:57.737 --> 00:02:59.657
"Hey, I'm having a baby."

00:03:00.820 --> 00:03:03.360
Or, "Look at what I did with my friends."

00:03:03.360 --> 00:03:07.220
And then it would be like, okay,
maybe once every four years

00:03:07.220 --> 00:03:10.290
during the presidential
election people might start

00:03:10.290 --> 00:03:13.020
like saying what they
stood for or whatever.

00:03:13.020 --> 00:03:17.060
But now it's like a really high percentage

00:03:17.060 --> 00:03:20.760
of the dialogue is just people
arguing with each other.

00:03:20.760 --> 00:03:23.540
But no one's actually moving
on anything, like you said,

00:03:23.540 --> 00:03:26.572
it's just people continuing to polarize

00:03:26.572 --> 00:03:29.780
and kind of align themselves

00:03:29.780 --> 00:03:31.837
with other people who all agree with them.

00:03:31.837 --> 00:03:36.060
And it's just, there's
no end in sight, right?

00:03:36.060 --> 00:03:36.960
It's discouraging.

00:03:36.960 --> 00:03:39.040
Yeah. I mean, so we're making a choice

00:03:39.040 --> 00:03:40.647
to not celebrate people who've said,

00:03:40.647 --> 00:03:42.930
"I changed someone else's mind."

00:03:42.930 --> 00:03:43.980
Yeah, right.

00:03:43.980 --> 00:03:45.900
"But I changed my own mind."

00:03:45.900 --> 00:03:47.130
And to see the things that lead

00:03:47.130 --> 00:03:48.520
to people changing their minds.

00:03:48.520 --> 00:03:50.180
And let me just say, of all,

00:03:50.180 --> 00:03:52.770
we read through almost all the responses,

00:03:52.770 --> 00:03:55.120
Kiko definitely read
through all the responses.

00:03:56.710 --> 00:03:58.947
None of them said,

00:03:58.947 --> 00:04:02.617
"I used to think this
and I changed my mind

00:04:02.617 --> 00:04:07.010
"because someone on
Facebook argued with me."

00:04:07.010 --> 00:04:09.480
That didn't happen in any one of these.

00:04:09.480 --> 00:04:11.580
Nobody said that.

00:04:11.580 --> 00:04:14.530
Right, yeah, so let's get into this.

00:04:14.530 --> 00:04:17.903
I think that we can start with Aleda.

00:04:19.340 --> 00:04:22.800
@aleda25543612.

00:04:22.800 --> 00:04:24.953
There's a lot of those already taken.

00:04:24.953 --> 00:04:27.780
Wow, there's a lot of Aleda's out there.

00:04:27.780 --> 00:04:32.360
250, 2,554,000.

00:04:32.360 --> 00:04:34.370
Nope, 25 million.

00:04:34.370 --> 00:04:35.590
There's 25 million.

00:04:35.590 --> 00:04:36.737
Is that a phone number?

00:04:36.737 --> 00:04:39.402
Can we put commas in
your username so we know

00:04:39.402 --> 00:04:40.939
what the number actually is?

00:04:40.939 --> 00:04:41.920
Let's call that number
and see if she answers.

00:04:41.920 --> 00:04:43.970
Oh, yeah, it's not a phone number?

00:04:43.970 --> 00:04:47.836
No, it's one, two, it's one less digit.

00:04:47.836 --> 00:04:50.010
We can probably figure it out.

00:04:50.010 --> 00:04:51.000
Yeah.
All right,

00:04:51.000 --> 00:04:52.620
we're calling an audible.

00:04:52.620 --> 00:04:54.633
This episode is now
devoted entirely to ...

00:04:54.633 --> 00:04:56.763
Trying to get in touch with Aleda.

00:04:59.290 --> 00:05:02.337
She responded to us at Mythical.

00:05:02.337 --> 00:05:05.247
"I absolutely hate the
sound of children singing."

00:05:07.600 --> 00:05:09.920
So you thought we were
gonna start all serious.

00:05:09.920 --> 00:05:11.260
No, we're not.

00:05:11.260 --> 00:05:12.333
It's gonna take us a
while to get through it.

00:05:12.333 --> 00:05:13.540
I don't know, I think
this is serious to her.

00:05:13.540 --> 00:05:16.107
She says, "I used to love it,

00:05:16.107 --> 00:05:19.407
"but then all those
singing idol shows came out

00:05:19.407 --> 00:05:22.297
"and now it's like
fingernails on a chalkboard.

00:05:22.297 --> 00:05:25.280
"P.S. I'm an elementary school teacher."

00:05:25.280 --> 00:05:26.440
Wow.

00:05:26.440 --> 00:05:31.440
So I wonder if it's the
culture of amateur singing

00:05:31.490 --> 00:05:34.470
in general that maybe inspired a change

00:05:34.470 --> 00:05:37.070
in the youth that then she
experienced personally?

00:05:37.070 --> 00:05:40.320
Or is it just the idea of seeing so many,

00:05:40.320 --> 00:05:41.470
I mean, how old do you have to be to be,

00:05:41.470 --> 00:05:44.900
you got to be like 13
to be on American Idol.

00:05:44.900 --> 00:05:47.730
Yeah, I think what she
means is that, you know,

00:05:47.730 --> 00:05:50.530
she's surrounded by these
elementary school kids

00:05:50.530 --> 00:05:53.190
who were like singing in
chorus and stuff like that.

00:05:53.190 --> 00:05:58.178
And it's what wasn't necessarily

00:05:58.178 --> 00:06:03.178
pro by any means or
splendid, but still cute,

00:06:04.060 --> 00:06:05.960
lost all of its luster

00:06:05.960 --> 00:06:08.467
to the point that she
actually said, "You know what?

00:06:08.467 --> 00:06:10.752
"I don't like kids singing anymore."

00:06:10.752 --> 00:06:13.406
Oh, I hate it, I hate the
sound of children singing.

00:06:13.406 --> 00:06:14.400
"You ruined it for me."

00:06:14.400 --> 00:06:17.810
Television idol, singing
idol shows ruined it for her,

00:06:17.810 --> 00:06:19.856
but she made a decision.

00:06:19.856 --> 00:06:21.850
She's like, "You know what?"

00:06:21.850 --> 00:06:25.280
Or, sometimes you come to
grips with how you've changed

00:06:25.280 --> 00:06:27.550
and you just, and so that's kind of like

00:06:27.550 --> 00:06:29.437
a backdoor decision, right?

00:06:29.437 --> 00:06:32.180
"Well, I don't like this
anymore and I'm okay with it."

00:06:32.180 --> 00:06:34.390
So the decision is owning it.

00:06:34.390 --> 00:06:38.260
Though it would make her life easier

00:06:38.260 --> 00:06:40.130
being an elementary school teacher

00:06:40.130 --> 00:06:41.930
if she would find a way to like it.

00:06:41.930 --> 00:06:44.290
Well, she's not, she's not the choral.

00:06:44.290 --> 00:06:47.437
She didn't say that, but if she said,

00:06:47.437 --> 00:06:50.191
"I am an elementary
school chorus teacher."

00:06:50.191 --> 00:06:51.859
Yeah, it would be tough.

00:06:51.859 --> 00:06:54.457
We would be outing her
right now, first of all.

00:06:54.457 --> 00:06:56.680
Well, she kind of outed
herself on Twitter.

00:06:56.680 --> 00:06:58.020
This makes me think of something.

00:06:58.020 --> 00:06:59.320
But it's not something you're supposed to,

00:06:59.320 --> 00:07:00.970
it's something you're supposed to like.

00:07:00.970 --> 00:07:04.270
So I do applaud her in
saying, "It is a preference."

00:07:04.270 --> 00:07:05.103
But she's standing by it.

00:07:05.103 --> 00:07:06.760
This is what I'm getting at is,

00:07:06.760 --> 00:07:11.350
I actually recently rediscovered

00:07:11.350 --> 00:07:13.787
how much I do like the
sound of children singing,

00:07:13.787 --> 00:07:16.250
and this sounds weird, I
understand, but let me just ...

00:07:16.250 --> 00:07:19.620
You talking about Christmas
boys' choir situation?

00:07:19.620 --> 00:07:21.780
I'm talking about Moonrise Kingdom,

00:07:21.780 --> 00:07:24.030
which I watched recently with the family.

00:07:24.030 --> 00:07:26.310
Me and Jesse and Shepherd watched it.

00:07:26.310 --> 00:07:30.510
And I'm beginning to really
kind of understand Shepherd

00:07:30.510 --> 00:07:33.290
and understand the kinds of
things that he will like.

00:07:33.290 --> 00:07:35.507
And so I was like,

00:07:35.507 --> 00:07:40.037
"Shepherd, I have a movie
that I want you to watch,

00:07:40.037 --> 00:07:43.221
"that I know you are going to like."

00:07:43.221 --> 00:07:46.983
Of course this is Wes
Anderson's Moonrise Kingdom.

00:07:46.983 --> 00:07:50.140
And right at the beginning,
I kind of look at him

00:07:50.140 --> 00:07:52.010
and he was just seeing the
way things were unfolding

00:07:52.010 --> 00:07:53.330
and the way things were being described.

00:07:53.330 --> 00:07:55.490
And he just had this
little smile on his face.

00:07:55.490 --> 00:07:57.900
And then he just kept looking

00:07:57.900 --> 00:08:00.880
at me and he was just like, "I love this."

00:08:00.880 --> 00:08:02.807
He was like, "This is the kind
of movie that I really like."

00:08:02.807 --> 00:08:04.003
And he wasn't just saying that

00:08:04.003 --> 00:08:07.350
because I had planted the
thought in his mind, but anyway.

00:08:07.350 --> 00:08:09.230
Great movie.
It's a great movie,

00:08:09.230 --> 00:08:13.550
and I feel kind of bad
about how much I didn't ...

00:08:13.550 --> 00:08:15.870
I loved it, I really liked it

00:08:15.870 --> 00:08:17.820
when I watched it in the theater.

00:08:17.820 --> 00:08:19.200
But I didn't realize that

00:08:20.090 --> 00:08:21.940
this might be my favorite
Wes Anderson movie.

00:08:21.940 --> 00:08:22.773
Oh really?

00:08:22.773 --> 00:08:26.273
Like it wasn't in like
the top three for me,

00:08:27.190 --> 00:08:28.970
but I feel like it is now.

00:08:28.970 --> 00:08:31.410
Anyway, lots of boys' choir.

00:08:31.410 --> 00:08:32.500
You changed your mind about it.

00:08:32.500 --> 00:08:34.360
Lots of boys' choir,

00:08:34.360 --> 00:08:35.980
which I've always kind of liked

00:08:35.980 --> 00:08:37.360
the sound of like a boys' choir.

00:08:37.360 --> 00:08:39.280
I've just thought that it's got this

00:08:39.280 --> 00:08:42.020
sort of angelic sound to it,

00:08:42.020 --> 00:08:44.950
but like the way that he
uses that music in the movie.

00:08:44.950 --> 00:08:47.840
There's a time and a place.
It's so good.

00:08:47.840 --> 00:08:48.740
It's just so good.

00:08:48.740 --> 00:08:50.600
So I like the sound of children singing.

00:08:50.600 --> 00:08:53.580
Well, you like the sound of
professional children singing.

00:08:53.580 --> 00:08:55.210
Yeah, yeah, right, not amateurs.

00:08:55.210 --> 00:08:56.860
Yeah.
Professional children.

00:08:58.300 --> 00:09:02.970
Kyna is the name of the next
respondent @therealkhaki.

00:09:05.037 --> 00:09:06.847
"I started drinking my coffee black

00:09:06.847 --> 00:09:08.157
"over the past couple of years.

00:09:08.157 --> 00:09:10.913
"It's important to assert
dominance over beverages.

00:09:10.913 --> 00:09:11.897
"#EarBiscuits."

00:09:13.060 --> 00:09:15.943
Wow, there's a lot to unpack here, Kyna.

00:09:19.218 --> 00:09:20.750
Because, man.

00:09:20.750 --> 00:09:23.440
The way that I kind of interpret this is

00:09:25.630 --> 00:09:28.830
I totally relate with the idea

00:09:28.830 --> 00:09:33.360
that there's something more respectable,

00:09:33.360 --> 00:09:34.960
and I'm not saying this is true.

00:09:34.960 --> 00:09:38.100
I'm saying I have this
perception that lives

00:09:38.100 --> 00:09:41.520
within my mind that there's
something more respectable

00:09:41.520 --> 00:09:45.350
about drinking coffee black,
that it's ultimately better.

00:09:45.350 --> 00:09:48.357
Like if you had to like rank five people

00:09:48.357 --> 00:09:50.857
and there was a person
who put a bunch of sugar

00:09:50.857 --> 00:09:53.080
and a bunch of cream in their coffee

00:09:53.080 --> 00:09:54.460
on one end of the spectrum,

00:09:54.460 --> 00:09:56.940
and there was a person who
drank their coffee black

00:09:56.940 --> 00:09:58.360
on the other end of the spectrum.

00:09:58.360 --> 00:10:01.250
I would respect the person
who drank their coffee black.

00:10:01.250 --> 00:10:04.290
And I don't exactly know
why that's the case.

00:10:04.290 --> 00:10:05.620
It's more hardcore.

00:10:05.620 --> 00:10:09.070
It's like it implies a toughness

00:10:09.070 --> 00:10:10.920
Because there's something
to protect yourself

00:10:10.920 --> 00:10:12.520
from when it comes to coffee.

00:10:12.520 --> 00:10:14.399
Because coffee is always bitter.

00:10:14.399 --> 00:10:16.617
It's an acquired taste.

00:10:16.617 --> 00:10:17.980
Yeah, when you add cream or sugar to it,

00:10:17.980 --> 00:10:21.520
as you take a sip of
your cream-laden coffee.

00:10:21.520 --> 00:10:22.353
Yeah, we'll get to that in a second.

00:10:22.353 --> 00:10:23.700
Any sugar in there?

00:10:23.700 --> 00:10:26.360
No sugar, but ...
I'm a no sugar.

00:10:26.360 --> 00:10:28.913
I'm a but cream man.
A butt cream man?

00:10:31.520 --> 00:10:32.680
I use butt cream as well.

00:10:32.680 --> 00:10:36.935
But every morning, every single morning,

00:10:36.935 --> 00:10:38.400
you know what, I'm realizing this.

00:10:38.400 --> 00:10:41.060
When I make my coffee

00:10:41.060 --> 00:10:44.200
and it's going down into my mug

00:10:44.200 --> 00:10:46.390
and then I go over to the fridge

00:10:46.390 --> 00:10:47.980
and I reach for the half-and-half,

00:10:47.980 --> 00:10:49.830
I have a pang of guilt

00:10:50.670 --> 00:10:55.670
to this day because, and because
of this sentiment of it's,

00:10:57.600 --> 00:11:02.600
you know, you're a bad-ass
if you can drink it black.

00:11:02.750 --> 00:11:03.650
And here I am, you know,

00:11:03.650 --> 00:11:06.562
it's like pouring defeat into my coffee.

00:11:06.562 --> 00:11:10.238
Yeah, okay, I have a theory about this.

00:11:10.238 --> 00:11:11.991
I think there's two
things going on, right?

00:11:11.991 --> 00:11:16.991
So the legitimate side
of the migration from

00:11:17.640 --> 00:11:19.240
a lot of sugar and a lot
of cream in your coffee

00:11:19.240 --> 00:11:21.000
to black coffee

00:11:21.000 --> 00:11:26.000
is a legitimate scientific
process that happens

00:11:26.560 --> 00:11:28.960
with things that are an
acquired taste, right?

00:11:28.960 --> 00:11:33.877
So it's like, "Oh, I
don't really like alcohol.

00:11:33.877 --> 00:11:34.967
"Oh, but you know what?

00:11:34.967 --> 00:11:39.967
"I had a wine cooler and
now I drink just white wine.

00:11:40.027 --> 00:11:41.677
"Oh, I'll drink just a white wine.

00:11:41.677 --> 00:11:42.810
"Well, you know what?

00:11:42.810 --> 00:11:45.007
"I tried a red, I tried a
Pinot Noir at this party

00:11:45.007 --> 00:11:45.840
"and it was pretty good."

00:11:45.840 --> 00:11:47.900
And then you work your way up
and the next thing you know

00:11:47.900 --> 00:11:51.670
you're just putting back
like 20 year aged scotch.

00:11:51.670 --> 00:11:53.100
You know what I'm saying?

00:11:53.100 --> 00:11:55.500
That's a scientific process of

00:11:55.500 --> 00:12:00.500
your literal face and mouth
adjusting to an acquired taste.

00:12:02.620 --> 00:12:04.290
And I think the same
thing happens with coffee.

00:12:04.290 --> 00:12:08.110
So, there's an achievement.
Right.

00:12:08.110 --> 00:12:09.623
You know, it's kind of
like winning at something.

00:12:09.623 --> 00:12:11.790
If you want to look at it that way.

00:12:11.790 --> 00:12:14.830
It's almost like there's
a purity of experience,

00:12:14.830 --> 00:12:16.360
especially when it comes to good coffee.

00:12:16.360 --> 00:12:19.080
That's why when you go to
a place like Intelligentsia

00:12:19.080 --> 00:12:22.700
and they've got like, you
know, an $8 pour over coffee

00:12:23.690 --> 00:12:24.920
and you ask for cream in it,

00:12:24.920 --> 00:12:28.077
they look at you with
disdain because they're like,

00:12:28.077 --> 00:12:29.977
"You're not, you're actually taking ..."

00:12:29.977 --> 00:12:30.887
"You're not woke."

00:12:30.887 --> 00:12:32.727
"Yeah, well, you're not
getting the experience

00:12:32.727 --> 00:12:36.317
"that was intended by the
person who made this coffee.

00:12:36.317 --> 00:12:37.717
"You're taking the edge off of something

00:12:37.717 --> 00:12:39.830
"that doesn't need the edge
off, this isn't Folgers."

00:12:39.830 --> 00:12:41.980
So I respect that.

00:12:41.980 --> 00:12:46.540
But I also feel like if you
like cream in your coffee

00:12:46.540 --> 00:12:49.589
more than you like black
coffee that trumps this,

00:12:49.589 --> 00:12:52.750
if you're not there yet or
you don't want to go there,

00:12:52.750 --> 00:12:56.520
listen, I tried for like
a year to do black coffee

00:12:56.520 --> 00:13:00.950
and I was, you know, I
had multiple motivations.

00:13:00.950 --> 00:13:02.500
One was like, "Okay, it's more healthy."

00:13:02.500 --> 00:13:04.870
'Cause there's no, you know,
there's less calories in there

00:13:04.870 --> 00:13:07.970
if it's just coffee, but
also it's more bad-ass.

00:13:07.970 --> 00:13:09.838
But then I just came to the conclusion

00:13:09.838 --> 00:13:11.750
I'm not gonna try anymore.

00:13:11.750 --> 00:13:13.890
And then during the pandemic

00:13:13.890 --> 00:13:18.890
I've become what I would
call a Cafe-Au-Lait-man.

00:13:18.900 --> 00:13:20.290
I mean, I'm basically ...

00:13:20.290 --> 00:13:22.390
You got that extra time to whip it up.

00:13:22.390 --> 00:13:25.820
I'm just saying that I'm about 50% milk,

00:13:25.820 --> 00:13:27.693
50% of coffee if I can be.

00:13:28.620 --> 00:13:30.970
And you know what?
I love everything about it.

00:13:32.470 --> 00:13:36.070
So you changed your mind
and I thought I had too,

00:13:36.070 --> 00:13:38.967
but I clearly haven't
fully changed my mind

00:13:38.967 --> 00:13:43.543
to accept my preference
and not judge myself.

00:13:44.818 --> 00:13:45.651
I think that, you know, there seems to be

00:13:45.651 --> 00:13:47.507
a tinge of pride in her post.

00:13:47.507 --> 00:13:50.030
"It's important to assert
dominance over beverages."

00:13:50.030 --> 00:13:51.440
Well, I think what she is doing ...

00:13:51.440 --> 00:13:52.390
She might have been baiting you,

00:13:52.390 --> 00:13:53.797
'cause I think this is
something that you ...

00:13:53.797 --> 00:13:56.643
Listen, she's obviously saying something

00:13:56.643 --> 00:13:58.483
slightly tongue in cheek,

00:13:59.330 --> 00:14:01.640
but I think it is reflective of

00:14:01.640 --> 00:14:04.350
that sort of cultural expectation

00:14:04.350 --> 00:14:07.410
of drinking black coffee
being more respectable.

00:14:07.410 --> 00:14:10.622
Which incidentally we've paired
this with another question.

00:14:10.622 --> 00:14:12.960
I'm sorry, before you do,

00:14:12.960 --> 00:14:15.650
I did want to acknowledge
that like when I was thinking

00:14:15.650 --> 00:14:18.440
of the ways that I've changed
my mind over the years,

00:14:18.440 --> 00:14:21.116
like I made a decision
to start drinking coffee

00:14:21.116 --> 00:14:24.400
in college because it's
like, "What am I missing?"

00:14:24.400 --> 00:14:25.654
And is it, you know,

00:14:25.654 --> 00:14:28.330
"Is this gonna be helpful
to keep me awake?"

00:14:28.330 --> 00:14:32.040
And you know, so I actually, I
changed my mind about coffee.

00:14:32.040 --> 00:14:34.030
You felt like you were becoming a, me too,

00:14:34.030 --> 00:14:35.820
we both started drinking
coffee in college.

00:14:35.820 --> 00:14:37.400
And I think many people do,

00:14:37.400 --> 00:14:39.390
because you're becoming
an independent adult

00:14:39.390 --> 00:14:40.713
and you're like, "This is what adults do."

00:14:40.713 --> 00:14:42.930
It's the same reason a lot of
people start drinking alcohol

00:14:42.930 --> 00:14:44.530
and don't like it at first.

00:14:44.530 --> 00:14:49.240
I don't think that that
social pressure is all bad

00:14:49.240 --> 00:14:50.680
or even bad at all.

00:14:50.680 --> 00:14:52.490
I think it's just a natural part of life.

00:14:52.490 --> 00:14:56.180
And you actually, sometimes
social pressure to mature

00:14:56.180 --> 00:14:59.281
and become an adult and
actually go through the process

00:14:59.281 --> 00:15:00.672
of acquiring a taste.

00:15:00.672 --> 00:15:02.187
There's some people who are like,

00:15:02.187 --> 00:15:05.457
"Okay, the whole acquired
taste thing is just bullshit,

00:15:05.457 --> 00:15:06.777
"because what you guys are saying is

00:15:06.777 --> 00:15:07.727
"that you don't really like it

00:15:07.727 --> 00:15:08.647
"but you're making yourself like it.

00:15:08.647 --> 00:15:10.430
"So I'm never going down that."

00:15:10.430 --> 00:15:12.551
But there's a reward at the end of

00:15:12.551 --> 00:15:14.530
an acquired taste journey.

00:15:14.530 --> 00:15:18.660
Yeah, someone else made
a post about whiskey.

00:15:18.660 --> 00:15:19.530
Exactly.

00:15:19.530 --> 00:15:21.264
It's a super common process.

00:15:21.264 --> 00:15:23.050
I don't think that's all bad,

00:15:23.050 --> 00:15:24.730
but there is a negative element to it,

00:15:24.730 --> 00:15:28.250
which you're kind of getting
at with judging yourself

00:15:28.250 --> 00:15:30.860
in the midst of the process, right?

00:15:30.860 --> 00:15:31.693
Mm-hmm.

00:15:31.693 --> 00:15:35.300
And that's where I think that
Arshia, mythical Zan Scaz,

00:15:38.370 --> 00:15:40.620
on Twitter is kind of
tapped into something.

00:15:40.620 --> 00:15:43.283
And this is what they have
changed their behavior about,

00:15:43.283 --> 00:15:44.977
changed their mind about.

00:15:44.977 --> 00:15:48.047
"My behavior, I'm a
textbook people-pleaser,

00:15:48.047 --> 00:15:51.487
"a very anxious person and
I have abandonment issues.

00:15:51.487 --> 00:15:54.227
"All my life I've tried
never to say no to someone,

00:15:54.227 --> 00:15:58.457
"tried to fit into social
norms and be the ideal person.

00:15:58.457 --> 00:15:59.647
"Now after therapy,

00:15:59.647 --> 00:16:03.480
"I prioritize myself
and my mental health."

00:16:03.480 --> 00:16:06.250
Thank you for sharing
this, Arsha or Arshia.

00:16:07.980 --> 00:16:11.370
This is by far the most
common response that we got

00:16:11.370 --> 00:16:13.700
to our particular post was, you know,

00:16:13.700 --> 00:16:16.163
related to the topic of ...

00:16:17.720 --> 00:16:19.360
Another way to put it is realizing

00:16:19.360 --> 00:16:22.550
that your life is your own.

00:16:22.550 --> 00:16:26.453
And if you start to trace the motives

00:16:26.453 --> 00:16:29.649
behind the decisions you're making,

00:16:29.649 --> 00:16:32.870
if you start pulling on those strings

00:16:32.870 --> 00:16:35.060
and they go out to other people

00:16:35.060 --> 00:16:38.950
or to just other ideas
outside of yourself,

00:16:38.950 --> 00:16:43.949
I think it can lead to problems, right?

00:16:43.949 --> 00:16:46.990
Well, it is an interesting balance, right?

00:16:46.990 --> 00:16:51.760
Because I do believe that
social pressure in general,

00:16:51.760 --> 00:16:55.070
there's a reason that
social pressure exists

00:16:55.070 --> 00:16:56.870
and there's a positive reason, right?

00:16:56.870 --> 00:17:00.190
We are a collective organism
whether we like it or not

00:17:00.190 --> 00:17:02.470
and whether Facebook seems
to reflect that or not,

00:17:02.470 --> 00:17:04.340
we are members of a community,

00:17:04.340 --> 00:17:05.430
we're members of a collective

00:17:05.430 --> 00:17:07.180
and there's a lot of aspects of

00:17:07.180 --> 00:17:10.580
our personalities and our biology

00:17:10.580 --> 00:17:13.610
that are kind of tuned
towards us getting along

00:17:13.610 --> 00:17:14.820
in the context of a group.

00:17:14.820 --> 00:17:17.070
That's where like the concept of shame,

00:17:17.070 --> 00:17:22.070
like the concept of shame
is not intrinsically bad

00:17:22.830 --> 00:17:24.460
from a biological perspective.

00:17:24.460 --> 00:17:26.680
It comes from something
that was actually useful,

00:17:26.680 --> 00:17:30.230
but in the modern world it's mostly toxic

00:17:30.230 --> 00:17:31.760
in the way that we experience it, right?

00:17:31.760 --> 00:17:33.333
And apply it.
I mean, yeah.

00:17:34.345 --> 00:17:37.750
So it does bear saying that
there are certain things

00:17:37.750 --> 00:17:40.490
that no one should get away with

00:17:40.490 --> 00:17:42.800
just because it's their prerogative.

00:17:42.800 --> 00:17:44.320
Right.
Right.

00:17:44.320 --> 00:17:46.460
There's a whole litany of those things.

00:17:46.460 --> 00:17:47.293
Yeah.

00:17:47.293 --> 00:17:49.329
But there are, you know,

00:17:49.329 --> 00:17:54.329
if you're a well-meaning person

00:17:55.120 --> 00:17:58.270
with a certain level of
humility that, you know,

00:17:58.270 --> 00:18:02.158
starts to understand themselves,
like this person is saying

00:18:02.158 --> 00:18:06.550
through therapy and understanding
that you've deprioritized

00:18:06.550 --> 00:18:08.480
yourself to the point that

00:18:11.260 --> 00:18:13.710
the fact that it's your life

00:18:13.710 --> 00:18:16.480
is not entering the decision matrix.

00:18:16.480 --> 00:18:18.160
Right, because when you make decisions

00:18:18.160 --> 00:18:20.860
that are about your health,

00:18:20.860 --> 00:18:22.330
prioritizing your mental health

00:18:22.330 --> 00:18:23.950
and making you a better person,

00:18:23.950 --> 00:18:26.940
you actually become a better
member of the collective.

00:18:26.940 --> 00:18:29.440
And you can, so you can,
it's a win-win situation

00:18:29.440 --> 00:18:31.320
when you understand yourself, right?

00:18:31.320 --> 00:18:33.310
And so I definitely, listen,

00:18:33.310 --> 00:18:37.757
I so relate to this,

00:18:37.757 --> 00:18:40.490
especially, I mean, the part about

00:18:40.490 --> 00:18:42.630
you know, being a people-pleaser,

00:18:42.630 --> 00:18:46.240
there's things that I
continue to unpack in therapy

00:18:46.240 --> 00:18:51.210
about just, you know, my personality

00:18:51.210 --> 00:18:52.710
and why my personality is what it is,

00:18:52.710 --> 00:18:54.900
but I am a people- pleaser.

00:18:54.900 --> 00:18:57.060
It's difficult to say no,

00:18:57.060 --> 00:19:02.060
I do have this sort of idea
of what is expected of me.

00:19:03.490 --> 00:19:07.560
And also a lot of times I
evaluate the things that I'm doing

00:19:07.560 --> 00:19:10.310
in the context of how I will
be perceived about them.

00:19:10.310 --> 00:19:13.385
And I have to continually
catch myself doing that

00:19:13.385 --> 00:19:15.917
and then be like, "Hold
on, are you doing this?

00:19:15.917 --> 00:19:20.027
"You're doing this for a bad
sort of like social reason

00:19:20.027 --> 00:19:21.397
"so you you'll be accepted

00:19:21.397 --> 00:19:23.217
"or people will perceive
you in a certain way,

00:19:23.217 --> 00:19:24.657
"or are you actually
thinking about yourself

00:19:24.657 --> 00:19:25.877
"and what you actually like

00:19:25.877 --> 00:19:27.980
"or what would be good for you?"

00:19:27.980 --> 00:19:30.860
You don't want to prioritize
yourself selfishly

00:19:30.860 --> 00:19:32.770
so that then it takes advantage of people,

00:19:32.770 --> 00:19:33.970
because that's a whole different,

00:19:33.970 --> 00:19:35.815
that's a completely
different concept, right?

00:19:35.815 --> 00:19:40.350
This is just about realizing when

00:19:40.350 --> 00:19:42.670
you don't need to be making a decision,

00:19:42.670 --> 00:19:45.080
because someone else's preference

00:19:45.080 --> 00:19:46.330
or someone else's perception is

00:19:46.330 --> 00:19:48.090
the thing that's driving you primarily.

00:19:48.090 --> 00:19:49.400
Yeah, you can become enslaved

00:19:49.400 --> 00:19:52.110
to other people's
expectations and opinions.

00:19:52.110 --> 00:19:55.520
Yeah.
And you can die inside.

00:19:55.520 --> 00:19:56.820
Yeah.

00:19:56.820 --> 00:19:59.820
And, but realizing that opens the door

00:19:59.820 --> 00:20:03.920
to changing your mind, you know?

00:20:03.920 --> 00:20:07.340
It's, you know, going back
to the beginning, you know,

00:20:07.340 --> 00:20:10.863
I think about the phrase of,

00:20:10.863 --> 00:20:13.930
it's like when, if somebody says something

00:20:13.930 --> 00:20:16.290
that makes you change your mind,

00:20:16.290 --> 00:20:18.800
I was thinking about the phrase,

00:20:18.800 --> 00:20:21.700
if you do want to say back to
them, "You changed my mind."

00:20:22.550 --> 00:20:26.960
And just the idea that, well, you know,

00:20:26.960 --> 00:20:30.430
sometimes you can realize
that your mind has changed.

00:20:30.430 --> 00:20:32.470
You can realize that
someone has had an influence

00:20:32.470 --> 00:20:35.407
over you and that it
could be a true statement,

00:20:35.407 --> 00:20:38.000
"You changed my mind."

00:20:38.000 --> 00:20:42.883
But it seems like the more
healthy disposition and approach

00:20:43.870 --> 00:20:45.397
is to say, "You know what?

00:20:45.397 --> 00:20:48.447
"I am the one who changes my mind.

00:20:48.447 --> 00:20:52.910
"I can choose whether I
allow someone's influence."

00:20:52.910 --> 00:20:55.890
Or, you know, not just
a person, an experience,

00:20:55.890 --> 00:20:59.660
whatever the case may be to influence me.

00:20:59.660 --> 00:21:02.140
But it's, you know,
giving someone the power,

00:21:02.140 --> 00:21:05.030
it's giving somebody power
over you if you're saying,

00:21:05.030 --> 00:21:07.610
you know, and maybe it's semantics,

00:21:07.610 --> 00:21:09.180
but saying, "You changed my mind."

00:21:09.180 --> 00:21:10.643
Versus ...
I think it's important.

00:21:10.643 --> 00:21:13.476
... "You helped me change my mind,

00:21:13.476 --> 00:21:15.900
"but I'm in charge."

00:21:15.900 --> 00:21:18.470
Again, this is my life.

00:21:18.470 --> 00:21:21.000
The decisions that I make,
the perspectives that I have,

00:21:21.000 --> 00:21:23.330
the actions that I take, you know,

00:21:23.330 --> 00:21:25.617
I'm ultimately culpable for
those, you just can't say,

00:21:25.617 --> 00:21:28.520
"Well, somebody told me to do it."

00:21:28.520 --> 00:21:30.050
Well, therapy's the
perfect example, right?

00:21:30.050 --> 00:21:33.481
Like that's why good
therapists and most therapists

00:21:33.481 --> 00:21:36.400
don't give advice

00:21:36.400 --> 00:21:38.350
and they don't tell you
what you should think.

00:21:38.350 --> 00:21:39.860
They ask questions and they lead you

00:21:39.860 --> 00:21:41.860
through a process of self discovery,

00:21:41.860 --> 00:21:43.800
because they understand that first of all,

00:21:43.800 --> 00:21:48.227
it ain't gonna last if it's not
a personal motivated change.

00:21:48.227 --> 00:21:49.490
And second of all, it's like

00:21:49.490 --> 00:21:50.660
they don't have the power to do it.

00:21:50.660 --> 00:21:52.600
Like you don't really have the power to

00:21:52.600 --> 00:21:53.710
change someone's mind.

00:21:53.710 --> 00:21:56.562
And this is coming from a person
who tries to be persuasive,

00:21:56.562 --> 00:22:01.290
has a very sort of, "I'm out
to change people's minds"

00:22:01.290 --> 00:22:04.700
natural disposition that I
have to kind of keep in check.

00:22:04.700 --> 00:22:06.590
Just reminding myself that

00:22:06.590 --> 00:22:08.464
people don't change their mind

00:22:08.464 --> 00:22:09.297
because of those kinds of things.

00:22:09.297 --> 00:22:10.150
Now, before we move on,

00:22:10.150 --> 00:22:12.170
we're gonna take short break in a second,

00:22:12.170 --> 00:22:13.563
but I do just want to, a related thing,

00:22:13.563 --> 00:22:15.300
just because I was thinking about it

00:22:15.300 --> 00:22:16.280
when we were talking about shame

00:22:16.280 --> 00:22:17.800
and like changing people's minds

00:22:17.800 --> 00:22:19.980
and social media and all
that stuff, is Brené Brown.

00:22:19.980 --> 00:22:21.490
I was listening to her recently

00:22:21.490 --> 00:22:23.100
and she was talking about how shame

00:22:23.100 --> 00:22:28.100
is not a good tool for social change.

00:22:32.090 --> 00:22:36.770
In other words, the way
to get someone to realize

00:22:36.770 --> 00:22:40.810
that what they think is wrong,
if that's what your goal is

00:22:40.810 --> 00:22:42.390
shame is not a great motivator.

00:22:42.390 --> 00:22:45.590
It actually doesn't do a lot
to change people's minds.

00:22:45.590 --> 00:22:47.250
It just makes them feel shameful,

00:22:47.250 --> 00:22:50.560
which then kind of, shame
usually leads to worse behavior,

00:22:50.560 --> 00:22:53.200
not like actual changing
and good behavior,

00:22:53.200 --> 00:22:55.040
but it seems that that's
something that happens

00:22:55.040 --> 00:22:56.260
on both sides of the,

00:22:56.260 --> 00:22:58.050
and I don't like to do the
whole both sides thing,

00:22:58.050 --> 00:23:01.860
but one of the things I've observed like

00:23:01.860 --> 00:23:04.910
as our country has gotten
even more polarized this year

00:23:04.910 --> 00:23:07.621
than it ever was in a
time when it seemed like

00:23:07.621 --> 00:23:10.000
we had a great opportunity
to kind of come together,

00:23:10.000 --> 00:23:11.113
we got more polarized.

00:23:12.220 --> 00:23:15.710
Both sides tend to point out
things about the other side,

00:23:15.710 --> 00:23:17.540
mischaracterize things
about the other side.

00:23:17.540 --> 00:23:19.727
And in the process, the goal is like,

00:23:19.727 --> 00:23:22.627
"I'm shaming you because you think this,

00:23:22.627 --> 00:23:25.458
"or you believe this or
you've identified in this way.

00:23:25.458 --> 00:23:28.670
"And that is shameful."

00:23:28.670 --> 00:23:30.810
It might be true that it's shameful.

00:23:30.810 --> 00:23:32.270
There are behaviors that are shameful.

00:23:32.270 --> 00:23:33.870
There are things that you can say and do

00:23:33.870 --> 00:23:36.770
and things that you can believe
that are truly shameful,

00:23:36.770 --> 00:23:39.700
but shaming someone about
them doesn't tend to

00:23:39.700 --> 00:23:42.240
be a great way to change
their minds about them.

