00:24:29.323 --> 00:24:31.247
Jade Marie posted,

00:24:31.247 --> 00:24:34.468
"I used to say that I
hated all country music

00:24:34.468 --> 00:24:37.455
"when I really just hated the uncle

00:24:37.455 --> 00:24:40.147
"who would always listen to country music.

00:24:40.147 --> 00:24:42.220
"No one wanted to hear
that opinion though."

00:24:42.220 --> 00:24:43.590
Yeah, again, this is a ...

00:24:43.590 --> 00:24:44.932
We did.
... this is a case.

00:24:44.932 --> 00:24:48.460
This is a case of your perception on,

00:24:48.460 --> 00:24:51.040
your perspective of
something is influenced

00:24:51.040 --> 00:24:53.830
by a relationship in a negative way.

00:24:53.830 --> 00:24:55.130
Yeah, I get it.

00:24:55.130 --> 00:24:57.132
I mean, didn't you, you told me ...

00:24:57.132 --> 00:24:57.965
But then you realize
you're making a decision.

00:24:57.965 --> 00:25:00.910
You told me a story, you
may have told them the story

00:25:00.910 --> 00:25:05.330
about how you made a
decision to not like music

00:25:05.330 --> 00:25:07.510
because your stepsister liked music.

00:25:07.510 --> 00:25:10.910
Yeah, when I was like in
grade school, I don't know.

00:25:10.910 --> 00:25:12.286
You know, it was just,

00:25:12.286 --> 00:25:15.653
it's not that we hated each
other, but there was this ...

00:25:15.653 --> 00:25:16.486
She was your stepsister.

00:25:16.486 --> 00:25:19.450
Stepsister, there was this
implied, I mean, we would argue

00:25:19.450 --> 00:25:22.483
we had to share a bathroom, you know?

00:25:22.483 --> 00:25:24.070
I didn't ...

00:25:24.070 --> 00:25:27.530
Let's see, she was probably, you know ...

00:25:27.530 --> 00:25:28.830
She was like a teenager.

00:25:28.830 --> 00:25:30.724
At least six, you know,

00:25:30.724 --> 00:25:31.830
maybe five years older than me, you know?

00:25:31.830 --> 00:25:34.060
And she, she was, she was not,

00:25:34.060 --> 00:25:36.940
she was just acting on teenage instinct.

00:25:36.940 --> 00:25:38.570
Well, you were thrust
into each other's lives

00:25:38.570 --> 00:25:40.302
at a really interesting time, right?

00:25:40.302 --> 00:25:42.937
Yeah, so I was like, "You know what?

00:25:42.937 --> 00:25:45.110
"I'm not gonna like
anything that she likes."

00:25:45.110 --> 00:25:47.170
Including music, and all music.

00:25:47.170 --> 00:25:48.730
Yeah.
It wasn't a genre.

00:25:48.730 --> 00:25:51.738
It was just like music as a thing.

00:25:51.738 --> 00:25:55.037
There was definitely, I'm sure
that that was a statement.

00:25:55.037 --> 00:25:56.277
"I don't like music."

00:25:57.460 --> 00:26:02.160
I mean, now music is
one of my top passions.

00:26:02.160 --> 00:26:03.510
Right.

00:26:03.510 --> 00:26:07.172
But, so there was a
point when it was like,

00:26:07.172 --> 00:26:09.360
I think once we started,

00:26:09.360 --> 00:26:12.157
once I started going over
to friends' homes and like,

00:26:12.157 --> 00:26:14.210
"Oh, they're really enjoying music.

00:26:14.210 --> 00:26:17.719
"I got to change my mind
about this, or I'm gonna be,

00:26:17.719 --> 00:26:20.210
"I'm gonna check-out of the zeitgeist."

00:26:20.210 --> 00:26:22.816
I think that those were the
terms I used as a third grader.

00:26:22.816 --> 00:26:23.770
Yeah, zeitgeist.

00:26:23.770 --> 00:26:27.377
It's like, "Man, I
gotta get this new tape.

00:26:27.377 --> 00:26:28.980
"I gotta start listening to tapes."

00:26:28.980 --> 00:26:29.913
Well, and specifically ...

00:26:29.913 --> 00:26:33.860
I changed my mind, I'm proud
to say I do like music now.

00:26:33.860 --> 00:26:36.080
A lot of people don't like country music

00:26:36.080 --> 00:26:37.620
because of the association.

00:26:37.620 --> 00:26:41.130
And, you know, whatever you
associate that with, right?

00:26:41.130 --> 00:26:43.547
If it's the parts about the
South that embarrass you,

00:26:43.547 --> 00:26:45.797
which there are parts about everywhere

00:26:45.797 --> 00:26:48.123
that embarrass everyone who's from them.

00:26:49.330 --> 00:26:50.579
And if it feels like ...

00:26:50.579 --> 00:26:52.540
Maybe it's just the
twangy earnestness of it.

00:26:52.540 --> 00:26:54.390
But I'm saying that it represents

00:26:54.390 --> 00:26:56.100
or is associated with a distillation

00:26:56.100 --> 00:26:58.940
of the things that
embarrass you about a place.

00:26:58.940 --> 00:26:59.990
There's a lot of people,

00:26:59.990 --> 00:27:01.796
we grew up with a lot
of people who are like,

00:27:01.796 --> 00:27:05.291
"I think that country
music isn't sophisticated

00:27:05.291 --> 00:27:07.920
"so I'm going to not like it."

00:27:07.920 --> 00:27:10.840
And sometimes you don't
even give it a chance.

00:27:10.840 --> 00:27:12.830
Now I will say, I went through a phase,

00:27:12.830 --> 00:27:14.100
now we always liked country music,

00:27:14.100 --> 00:27:17.297
but we always liked sort
of classic country music.

00:27:17.297 --> 00:27:19.340
And there were a few
sort of modern artists.

00:27:19.340 --> 00:27:22.090
Like I went, I was
really into Garth Brooks

00:27:22.090 --> 00:27:23.800
in the early '90s.

00:27:23.800 --> 00:27:27.320
I did not follow him into
the Chris Gaines era, okay?

00:27:27.320 --> 00:27:28.840
I'm proud to say that I didn't do that.

00:27:28.840 --> 00:27:31.450
Not because it wasn't country,
just because it was horrible.

00:27:31.450 --> 00:27:35.530
But I think that, and
then as an adult, like,

00:27:35.530 --> 00:27:37.590
even like right after
Jesse and I got married,

00:27:37.590 --> 00:27:40.220
we went through a couple of years phase

00:27:40.220 --> 00:27:43.990
where we would listen
to like 94.7 in Raleigh,

00:27:43.990 --> 00:27:46.130
like the modern country.
Modern country.

00:27:46.130 --> 00:27:49.847
And it was so positive and so sweet

00:27:49.847 --> 00:27:52.300
that there was this
sort of like young love,

00:27:52.300 --> 00:27:54.876
married, starting a family
kind of vibe that country music

00:27:54.876 --> 00:27:59.876
sort of just un-ironically
embraces that we liked.

00:28:00.113 --> 00:28:01.892
Now when we go back,

00:28:01.892 --> 00:28:05.190
I don't listen to much modern
country at all right now.

00:28:05.190 --> 00:28:06.430
Yeah.

00:28:06.430 --> 00:28:08.490
I mean, I love Jason Isbell
and Sturgill Simpson,

00:28:08.490 --> 00:28:11.040
but they don't really,

00:28:11.040 --> 00:28:13.320
they're not considered country

00:28:13.320 --> 00:28:15.309
by a lot of the country people.

00:28:15.309 --> 00:28:18.290
But when we go back to North Carolina

00:28:18.290 --> 00:28:20.630
one of the things that I do as a habit is

00:28:20.630 --> 00:28:23.760
we listen to local country radio

00:28:23.760 --> 00:28:25.270
whenever we drive in the car

00:28:25.270 --> 00:28:28.927
just to like kind of
just dip in completely.

00:28:28.927 --> 00:28:32.375
And it's difficult for me
to take, I gotta be honest.

00:28:32.375 --> 00:28:34.450
Yeah.
It's pretty difficult.

00:28:34.450 --> 00:28:37.790
But like eradicating and writing off

00:28:37.790 --> 00:28:42.050
an entire genre of all
country music, or, you know

00:28:42.050 --> 00:28:44.660
someone made the same post,
but about electronic music,

00:28:44.660 --> 00:28:47.673
and then started, described how they

00:28:47.673 --> 00:28:50.372
began to understand the
craftsmanship around it.

00:28:50.372 --> 00:28:54.250
And they changed their mind about the,

00:28:54.250 --> 00:28:57.550
well, true music has to be created

00:28:57.550 --> 00:29:01.260
with actual like acoustic instruments.

00:29:01.260 --> 00:29:02.548
Yeah.

00:29:02.548 --> 00:29:04.100
Or, you know, like playable instruments.

00:29:04.100 --> 00:29:06.500
And even that, I don't know,

00:29:06.500 --> 00:29:09.430
they say the dichotomy a
little bit differently,

00:29:09.430 --> 00:29:11.618
because you would feel,
sense and things like that

00:29:11.618 --> 00:29:12.780
Yeah, a big boomer perspective,

00:29:12.780 --> 00:29:14.420
like the way boomers think about rap,

00:29:14.420 --> 00:29:17.220
like, you know, I've heard many boomers

00:29:17.220 --> 00:29:20.960
talk about rap as if rap
doesn't take any talent, right?

00:29:20.960 --> 00:29:22.927
Like, "Oh, these guys
are just standing there,

00:29:22.927 --> 00:29:24.387
"and like go see him in concert.

00:29:24.387 --> 00:29:26.570
"There's just music playing
and they're walking around."

00:29:26.570 --> 00:29:27.403
Right.

00:29:27.403 --> 00:29:29.470
I think we have an appreciation for it,

00:29:29.470 --> 00:29:33.568
because we've tried to do
it in parody form, you know?

00:29:33.568 --> 00:29:35.707
We've tried to be like,

00:29:35.707 --> 00:29:37.670
"All right, we're gonna do this rap."

00:29:37.670 --> 00:29:42.670
And when you recognize how hard
it is to make it sound good

00:29:44.350 --> 00:29:45.850
or make it sound anywhere close to what

00:29:45.850 --> 00:29:46.700
a professional would do,

00:29:46.700 --> 00:29:48.490
you start realizing that this is,

00:29:48.490 --> 00:29:49.960
same thing with like,

00:29:49.960 --> 00:29:52.330
I've always had this thing about poetry,

00:29:52.330 --> 00:29:57.330
which is like, "Okay, so
what's the deal with poetry?

00:29:58.487 --> 00:30:00.438
"Especially poetry that doesn't rhyme."

00:30:00.438 --> 00:30:03.000
You know what I mean?

00:30:03.000 --> 00:30:04.248
It just, yeah.

00:30:04.248 --> 00:30:07.956
there's a couple of, a smattering of words

00:30:07.956 --> 00:30:11.010
and a random indention,

00:30:11.010 --> 00:30:12.430
and then another smattering of words,

00:30:12.430 --> 00:30:13.650
then it's over.

00:30:13.650 --> 00:30:16.670
I've lived long enough to know

00:30:16.670 --> 00:30:19.830
that if I were really to dive
into the world of poetry,

00:30:19.830 --> 00:30:22.700
I would be like, "I see
what makes a good poet."

00:30:22.700 --> 00:30:26.300
I gotta say, I'm an
unsophisticated country boy

00:30:26.300 --> 00:30:27.730
from North Carolina in a lot of ways.

00:30:27.730 --> 00:30:29.630
And one of those ways
is that my favorite poet

00:30:29.630 --> 00:30:32.070
is Shel Silverstein, right?

00:30:32.070 --> 00:30:34.853
Happy to say it, but I'm
sure that if I understood

00:30:34.853 --> 00:30:39.350
like the reality of what
makes Emily Dickinson good

00:30:40.820 --> 00:30:42.544
that I would be like,

00:30:42.544 --> 00:30:43.847
"Oh, this isn't just something
that somebody determined.

00:30:43.847 --> 00:30:47.457
"Like there's actually a reason for this.

00:30:47.457 --> 00:30:48.777
"And I couldn't do it.

00:30:48.777 --> 00:30:51.010
"I couldn't just start writing things."

00:30:51.010 --> 00:30:53.920
Everybody thinks that they
can write poetry, right?

00:30:53.920 --> 00:30:54.753
Yeah.

00:30:54.753 --> 00:30:57.383
But that's not how it
works, it's more complex.

00:30:58.771 --> 00:31:02.990
I think that's all I have
to say about country music.

00:31:02.990 --> 00:31:07.990
John Mueller gets, you know,
gets really honest here.

00:31:08.490 --> 00:31:10.047
In fact, begins to post with,

00:31:10.047 --> 00:31:11.673
"Honestly, y'all ...

00:31:14.130 --> 00:31:16.240
The way he changed his mind

00:31:16.240 --> 00:31:18.080
was related to Black Lives Matter.

00:31:18.080 --> 00:31:20.557
He says, "Honestly,
y'all, Black Lives Matter.

00:31:20.557 --> 00:31:24.280
"I was one of those dumb people
who got mad at Kaepernick."

00:31:24.280 --> 00:31:25.769
Kaepernick.

00:31:25.769 --> 00:31:26.724
I'm gonna say that wrong.

00:31:26.724 --> 00:31:28.760
When I see it, I'm like,
"I'm gonna say it wrong."

00:31:28.760 --> 00:31:30.317
Well, you did.
"... Kaepernick

00:31:30.317 --> 00:31:33.297
"about his protest, but what
happened throughout last year

00:31:33.297 --> 00:31:36.917
"really galvanized a 180
degree change for me.

00:31:36.917 --> 00:31:41.917
"Hat tip to Michael Che Matters
and Dave Chappelle as well."

00:31:42.390 --> 00:31:43.820
Hmm.

00:31:43.820 --> 00:31:44.653
Yeah, again.

00:31:44.653 --> 00:31:45.950
Thanks John, for sharing that.

00:31:45.950 --> 00:31:47.980
Yeah, thanks for being
honest and vulnerable,

00:31:47.980 --> 00:31:48.910
you know, publicly.

00:31:48.910 --> 00:31:52.160
It's not something that happens
a lot, especially, you know

00:31:52.160 --> 00:31:53.750
you don't hear it a whole lot from

00:31:53.750 --> 00:31:56.930
white guys who say y'all, you know?

00:31:56.930 --> 00:31:59.580
That this is something that
they changed their minds about.

00:31:59.580 --> 00:32:00.740
So thanks for doing that.

00:32:00.740 --> 00:32:05.020
I think that, you know,
we feel like we can speak

00:32:05.020 --> 00:32:06.380
with some authority on this issue,

00:32:06.380 --> 00:32:09.440
because we are white boys

00:32:09.440 --> 00:32:13.300
who grew up in the South
and had perspective

00:32:13.300 --> 00:32:16.510
on these things that we don't think

00:32:16.510 --> 00:32:19.280
it was really based in
reality and was ultimately,

00:32:19.280 --> 00:32:21.930
could ultimately be
described as racist, right?

00:32:21.930 --> 00:32:26.930
Not in your classic, external
explicit expressions of racism

00:32:30.370 --> 00:32:32.396
that you'd see in like a
movie about the deep South,

00:32:32.396 --> 00:32:37.380
but more this, this is
something that is in the fabric

00:32:37.380 --> 00:32:39.130
of the way that we think
and we see the world

00:32:39.130 --> 00:32:41.409
that ultimately is rooted in this idea

00:32:41.409 --> 00:32:44.585
that whiteness is the standard
and whiteness is normal.

00:32:44.585 --> 00:32:48.640
And it's, you know, it's taken
years to deconstruct that,

00:32:48.640 --> 00:32:51.470
deconstruct that and to see all the places

00:32:51.470 --> 00:32:52.474
that it impacted and all the ways

00:32:52.474 --> 00:32:54.720
that it impacted our thinking.

00:32:54.720 --> 00:32:55.850
I mean, I talked a little bit about this

00:32:55.850 --> 00:32:58.338
in my letter to a white man that I wrote.

00:32:58.338 --> 00:32:59.590
A lot about it.

00:32:59.590 --> 00:33:00.423
That was the point of it.

00:33:00.423 --> 00:33:02.690
Yeah, that was the point, was
to talk to my former self.

00:33:02.690 --> 00:33:05.777
But I mean, in general, when you say,

00:33:05.777 --> 00:33:07.550
"I changed my mind about blank."

00:33:07.550 --> 00:33:09.640
But then specifically if it's

00:33:09.640 --> 00:33:14.110
I changed my mind about systemic racism

00:33:15.850 --> 00:33:19.919
and, or the Black Lives
Matter movement, you know?

00:33:19.919 --> 00:33:22.630
That's a loaded statement, right?

00:33:22.630 --> 00:33:25.440
Because it implies that
you're coming from a place

00:33:25.440 --> 00:33:30.440
that is rooted in the opposite, rooted in,

00:33:30.660 --> 00:33:34.360
as you just said, like in racism.

00:33:34.360 --> 00:33:35.290
Yeah.

00:33:35.290 --> 00:33:38.900
Maybe it's not on the surface,
but if you trace it back

00:33:38.900 --> 00:33:42.970
you have those tendencies
and it's been baked

00:33:42.970 --> 00:33:47.970
into your psyche and you've
got to scrape it out over time,

00:33:49.449 --> 00:33:52.270
so there's a confession in there.

00:33:52.270 --> 00:33:53.150
For real, yeah.
You know?

00:33:53.150 --> 00:33:56.330
So that's why I applaud John.

00:33:56.330 --> 00:34:00.750
John, whenever you say you
change your mind about something

00:34:00.750 --> 00:34:04.985
there's all there is, it's
that level of humility to say,

00:34:04.985 --> 00:34:09.287
especially on this topic that like,

00:34:09.287 --> 00:34:13.814
"I was wrong about one of
the things that like ... "

00:34:13.814 --> 00:34:15.437
You know, if you could just say

00:34:15.437 --> 00:34:19.450
"I adopt, yes, I support
Black Lives Matter."

00:34:19.450 --> 00:34:22.370
And you just say only
the positive, you know

00:34:22.370 --> 00:34:26.887
maybe that's great, but to say,

00:34:26.887 --> 00:34:29.237
"I'm coming to grips with
what I used to believe

00:34:29.237 --> 00:34:31.238
"and I'm changing."

00:34:31.238 --> 00:34:34.633
I think that humility is called for,

00:34:37.040 --> 00:34:39.780
it has so much more of an
impact on others as well.

00:34:39.780 --> 00:34:40.890
It's much more powerful.

00:34:40.890 --> 00:34:45.890
I mean, listen, the
reason I put that article,

00:34:47.380 --> 00:34:49.420
I wrote that article in
that way for two reasons,

00:34:49.420 --> 00:34:50.767
one is I wasn't trying to be like,

00:34:50.767 --> 00:34:52.720
"Hey, I'm an authority on this."

00:34:52.720 --> 00:34:55.960
It was more like my
primary goal this year,

00:34:55.960 --> 00:34:57.229
as it relates to these issues is just

00:34:57.229 --> 00:34:59.310
to listen and learn, because I don't,

00:34:59.310 --> 00:35:00.530
I'm not as well educated

00:35:00.530 --> 00:35:02.890
and I don't have the personal experience

00:35:02.890 --> 00:35:06.040
of people of color and
what they've gone through.

00:35:06.040 --> 00:35:07.340
But the second thing was

00:35:07.340 --> 00:35:11.830
this is the only thing that
might have been meaningful

00:35:11.830 --> 00:35:14.099
and impactful to me in the
past is just hearing someone

00:35:14.099 --> 00:35:18.520
that I could relate to talk
about these things, right?

00:35:18.520 --> 00:35:21.408
And that's why I kind of wrote
the letter to my former self.

00:35:21.408 --> 00:35:26.408
But this is a really difficult
thing to change your mind on

00:35:26.600 --> 00:35:30.900
especially when you've got lots of people

00:35:30.900 --> 00:35:33.790
in your community and
in the world, frankly,

00:35:33.790 --> 00:35:38.470
who find all kinds of ways to
discredit the movement, right?

00:35:38.470 --> 00:35:40.137
So it might be anything from like,

00:35:40.137 --> 00:35:42.267
"Okay, well let me tell you
what Black Lives Matter did,

00:35:42.267 --> 00:35:45.897
"Black Lives Matter was
responsible for violent, you know,

00:35:45.897 --> 00:35:48.810
"riots or however you want to, you know?"

00:35:48.810 --> 00:35:52.380
So saying that because there was violence

00:35:52.380 --> 00:35:55.550
or that property damage or
whatever that took place

00:35:55.550 --> 00:35:59.050
as in conjunction with the BLM protest,

00:35:59.050 --> 00:36:00.930
therefore the underlying thing

00:36:00.930 --> 00:36:02.630
that's being fought for is illegitimate,

00:36:02.630 --> 00:36:04.370
and you can just write
the whole thing off.

00:36:04.370 --> 00:36:07.817
Or you can mischaracterize the movement as

00:36:07.817 --> 00:36:11.317
"This is just a Marxist
movement that's designed to

00:36:11.317 --> 00:36:13.957
"completely rip apart the
fabric of traditional America

00:36:13.957 --> 00:36:16.467
"and therefore I'm going
to, again, write off,

00:36:16.467 --> 00:36:18.727
"I'm not going to entertain
any empathy for this

00:36:18.727 --> 00:36:20.577
"and I'm not going to try to address

00:36:20.577 --> 00:36:23.530
"the central issue that's
being talked about."

00:36:23.530 --> 00:36:26.670
There's so many sort of
corners that you can crawl into

00:36:26.670 --> 00:36:29.530
to avoid actually dealing with

00:36:29.530 --> 00:36:33.300
the central issue of the
history of systemic racism

00:36:33.300 --> 00:36:35.140
and the way that it
has morphed and changed

00:36:35.140 --> 00:36:38.367
and gotten even more sort of conniving

00:36:38.367 --> 00:36:40.870
in the more recent past
and not as obvious,

00:36:40.870 --> 00:36:43.610
which makes it even harder to sniff out,

00:36:43.610 --> 00:36:47.920
but it just takes,
honestly, it takes a lot.

00:36:47.920 --> 00:36:51.842
It takes a lot for people to
experience this kind of change.

00:36:51.842 --> 00:36:54.591
And we personally have a lot of empathy

00:36:54.591 --> 00:36:59.156
for the people who are
resistant to that change

00:36:59.156 --> 00:37:01.140
and are experiencing that change.

00:37:01.140 --> 00:37:03.740
So, you know, again, like Link said,

00:37:03.740 --> 00:37:06.010
kudos to John for sharing this,

00:37:06.010 --> 00:37:07.730
because it's really the only way

00:37:07.730 --> 00:37:09.040
that we can see real change

00:37:09.040 --> 00:37:12.720
is the people who are
holding back the change

00:37:12.720 --> 00:37:14.713
are actually experiencing the change.

00:37:16.120 --> 00:37:21.120
And I just think that it's powerful.

00:37:21.370 --> 00:37:25.960
And it just bears saying
again, because it's, you know

00:37:25.960 --> 00:37:28.428
it might be easy for us just to say

00:37:28.428 --> 00:37:32.190
all the positive things
and to say the right things

00:37:32.190 --> 00:37:34.610
about the Black Lives Matter movement now,

00:37:34.610 --> 00:37:37.630
because we are in front
of these microphones

00:37:37.630 --> 00:37:41.620
and you could argue that
we have something to lose

00:37:41.620 --> 00:37:44.870
by saying the wrong thing.
Yeah.

00:37:44.870 --> 00:37:46.150
I hope you know that's not what's going on

00:37:46.150 --> 00:37:49.390
and that we're, you know,
we're sincere about this,

00:37:49.390 --> 00:37:54.070
but part of that is saying
we've had to change.

00:37:54.070 --> 00:37:56.287
You know, I think that's kind
of the proof in it is saying,

00:37:56.287 --> 00:37:57.607
"Well, it's not that I've always been

00:37:57.607 --> 00:37:58.657
"on the right side of this.

00:37:58.657 --> 00:38:00.884
"I've been, I was, you know,

00:38:00.884 --> 00:38:04.010
"I grew up on the wrong side of this."

00:38:04.010 --> 00:38:04.843
Yeah.

00:38:05.720 --> 00:38:09.930
And so making that change
is nothing to brag about.

00:38:09.930 --> 00:38:13.360
It's something that instinctively
I'd rather not talk about.

00:38:13.360 --> 00:38:14.193
Yeah.

00:38:14.193 --> 00:38:16.360
But again, that's why in general

00:38:16.360 --> 00:38:21.310
we're championing people who
say, "I changed my mind."

00:38:21.310 --> 00:38:23.280
Yeah.
Yeah.

00:38:23.280 --> 00:38:26.950
Which implies that like John was saying

00:38:26.950 --> 00:38:29.800
that 180 degree turn.

00:38:29.800 --> 00:38:31.850
Well, and I think it's
significant that, you know,

00:38:31.850 --> 00:38:34.337
he points out Michael Che
and Dave Chappelle, right?

00:38:34.337 --> 00:38:37.790
And you know, both of
those guys tend to be

00:38:37.790 --> 00:38:40.170
pretty polarizing figures
in their own right, right?

00:38:40.170 --> 00:38:41.010
You take Dave Chappelle,

00:38:41.010 --> 00:38:42.610
Dave Chappelle says a lot of things

00:38:42.610 --> 00:38:44.720
that are offensive to a lot of people.

00:38:44.720 --> 00:38:48.293
He tends to be a very
polarizing character,

00:38:49.930 --> 00:38:53.367
but I find it interesting that
somebody like John would say,

00:38:53.367 --> 00:38:56.229
"Well, you know, Dave Chappelle
was actually responsible,

00:38:56.229 --> 00:38:59.687
"partially responsible
for me changing my mind

00:38:59.687 --> 00:39:01.310
"about this central issue."

00:39:01.310 --> 00:39:03.860
And I think, again, that goes to that,

00:39:03.860 --> 00:39:06.110
this is not some clean process.

00:39:06.110 --> 00:39:08.580
There's not just this, you know,

00:39:08.580 --> 00:39:10.860
everybody who thinks right
about this is on this side

00:39:10.860 --> 00:39:13.610
and everybody thinks wrong
about it is on this side.

00:39:13.610 --> 00:39:16.690
And you have to be, you have
to talk about this stuff

00:39:16.690 --> 00:39:18.610
and you have to be right
in every single way

00:39:18.610 --> 00:39:19.750
that you talk about it.

00:39:19.750 --> 00:39:21.937
And you have to be
super, super consistent.

00:39:21.937 --> 00:39:26.937
When we insist on this
absolute standard of excellence

00:39:27.180 --> 00:39:28.838
and complete wokeness

00:39:28.838 --> 00:39:31.990
in order to accept people,

00:39:31.990 --> 00:39:33.390
one of the things that we do is

00:39:33.390 --> 00:39:36.130
we lose the ultimate mission,

00:39:36.130 --> 00:39:39.010
which is not that we all just
think exactly the same way

00:39:39.010 --> 00:39:40.570
and agree on every single thing

00:39:40.570 --> 00:39:42.910
and have some standard that

00:39:42.910 --> 00:39:44.600
we hold ourselves accountable to.

00:39:44.600 --> 00:39:46.100
No, we've got a whole lot of people

00:39:46.100 --> 00:39:48.240
that we're trying to move
in the right direction.

00:39:48.240 --> 00:39:50.660
The goal is for all of us
to collectively get better,

00:39:50.660 --> 00:39:53.602
and that you have to take
into account process.

00:39:53.602 --> 00:39:54.730
Right.

00:39:54.730 --> 00:39:57.650
And ethicacy, and I think
people might change.

00:39:57.650 --> 00:40:00.560
I think, honestly, I'm
not saying that, again,

00:40:00.560 --> 00:40:02.990
me saying that I think Dave Chappelle

00:40:02.990 --> 00:40:05.280
has been a force for good
in this particular regard

00:40:05.280 --> 00:40:06.600
as John is pointing out,

00:40:06.600 --> 00:40:09.470
does not mean that I endorse
all of Dave Chappelle's comedy

00:40:09.470 --> 00:40:13.223
or all of Dave Chappelle's perspectives.

00:40:14.060 --> 00:40:17.500
But all I'm saying is
that I don't have to agree

00:40:17.500 --> 00:40:19.760
with every single thing
that somebody does or says

00:40:19.760 --> 00:40:20.910
in order to be able to say that

00:40:20.910 --> 00:40:23.900
they've been a force for good
about this particular issue

00:40:23.900 --> 00:40:24.917
and be like, "You know what?

00:40:24.917 --> 00:40:27.407
"You did some good work
there and that's great.

00:40:27.407 --> 00:40:28.722
"You changed somebody's mind."

00:40:28.722 --> 00:40:30.180
That's what we're trying to do.

00:40:30.180 --> 00:40:33.160
We're trying to change peoples' mind.

00:40:33.160 --> 00:40:34.060
We're trying to, like you said,

00:40:34.060 --> 00:40:36.480
become better as a collective.

00:40:36.480 --> 00:40:37.997
We're not just trying to be like,

00:40:37.997 --> 00:40:40.197
"Here's the fence between the two sides

00:40:40.197 --> 00:40:41.157
"and I want to make sure that

00:40:41.157 --> 00:40:43.087
"I keep putting up boards on that fence

00:40:43.087 --> 00:40:44.917
"and make sure everybody's
over here on this side.

00:40:44.917 --> 00:40:45.987
"And let's keep building that fence

00:40:45.987 --> 00:40:46.870
"higher and higher and higher."

00:40:46.870 --> 00:40:48.480
No, what we want to do is

00:40:48.480 --> 00:40:50.460
we want to bring the
fence down lower and lower

00:40:50.460 --> 00:40:52.460
and continue to bring
people over it, you know?

00:40:52.460 --> 00:40:55.440
I would much rather be
friends with someone

00:40:55.440 --> 00:41:00.440
who was not on the right side of an issue,

00:41:01.650 --> 00:41:05.480
but demonstrated a
sincere open mind to it.

00:41:05.480 --> 00:41:06.313
Yeah.

00:41:06.313 --> 00:41:10.330
Than someone who believed
all the right things

00:41:10.330 --> 00:41:13.960
and was firmly in the right.

00:41:13.960 --> 00:41:16.700
If, you know, if you're
able to assess that by,

00:41:16.700 --> 00:41:19.780
from any vantage point.

00:41:19.780 --> 00:41:24.780
You know, it's just that
that's not, there's no grace.

00:41:24.840 --> 00:41:28.940
That's not a reflection of
humanity and how, you know,

00:41:28.940 --> 00:41:33.040
the human experience of, you know,

00:41:33.040 --> 00:41:34.740
everybody's somewhere and

00:41:34.740 --> 00:41:37.170
is not going to be right
about every everything.

00:41:37.170 --> 00:41:38.505
And they're not ...

00:41:38.505 --> 00:41:39.800
You're gonna be wrong about most things.

00:41:39.800 --> 00:41:41.000
You're gonna be wrong about most things.

00:41:41.000 --> 00:41:43.740
Everyone is going to be
wrong about most things.

00:41:43.740 --> 00:41:47.210
It's easy to put it in the poetry example.

00:41:47.210 --> 00:41:50.151
Again, you know, if
someone said, you know,

00:41:50.151 --> 00:41:52.790
"I don't get poetry."

00:41:52.790 --> 00:41:53.623
It's like, "You know what?"

00:41:53.623 --> 00:41:55.640
And if I was a poetry scholar ...

00:41:55.640 --> 00:41:56.880
You could have been.

00:41:56.880 --> 00:41:58.600
Or let's say that's what I
was most passionate about.

00:41:58.600 --> 00:42:00.261
Could have been.
It's not ...

00:42:00.261 --> 00:42:01.094
You have glasses.

00:42:01.094 --> 00:42:02.673
I'm with you, I don't get poetry.

00:42:02.673 --> 00:42:05.088
But I just wouldn't,

00:42:05.088 --> 00:42:08.020
I don't know that I would
want to converse with someone

00:42:08.020 --> 00:42:10.730
who says, "You know, I hate poetry."

00:42:10.730 --> 00:42:11.740
Right.
You know?

00:42:11.740 --> 00:42:12.573
It's just ...

00:42:12.573 --> 00:42:16.081
Versus I don't think I
understand poetry enough.

00:42:16.081 --> 00:42:17.091
I hate jazz.

00:42:17.091 --> 00:42:18.220
I don't think I understand poetry enough

00:42:18.220 --> 00:42:19.280
to really appreciate it.

00:42:19.280 --> 00:42:20.730
That's one way to say it.

00:42:20.730 --> 00:42:21.910
You know what I mean?

00:42:21.910 --> 00:42:22.743
And so, yeah.

00:42:22.743 --> 00:42:25.037
And again, I think that we talk about,

00:42:25.037 --> 00:42:27.968
we talk about these things
in a way that may be,

00:42:27.968 --> 00:42:32.250
again, it's, what I am saying is

00:42:32.250 --> 00:42:36.330
that I have empathy for
people who disagree with me

00:42:36.330 --> 00:42:39.020
because I disagree with my past self.

00:42:39.020 --> 00:42:40.440
Right.
It's as simple as that.

00:42:40.440 --> 00:42:44.360
And I don't know, I get so frustrated

00:42:44.360 --> 00:42:46.720
with the nature of dialogue

00:42:46.720 --> 00:42:48.800
on both the left and the right,

00:42:48.800 --> 00:42:53.800
because it's as if the goal
is to solidify the ranks

00:42:55.100 --> 00:42:57.740
versus actually make progress

00:42:57.740 --> 00:43:00.180
and move the collective
in the right direction.

00:43:00.180 --> 00:43:01.977
And I don't know, I just feel like,

00:43:01.977 --> 00:43:04.127
'cause we get into a place
where it's just like,

00:43:04.127 --> 00:43:07.567
"Oh, that person said this
thing that offended me.

00:43:07.567 --> 00:43:10.107
"And therefore every single
thing they've ever done

00:43:10.107 --> 00:43:13.110
"or will ever do is illegitimate."

00:43:13.110 --> 00:43:14.260
It's like, well,

00:43:14.260 --> 00:43:16.940
I don't want to treat myself in that way.

00:43:16.940 --> 00:43:18.400
I don't want to treat anybody in that way.

00:43:18.400 --> 00:43:20.131
I wouldn't want to be treated that way.

00:43:20.131 --> 00:43:21.620
Because I'm gonna be wrong.

00:43:21.620 --> 00:43:22.970
I'm gonna say things that are wrong.

00:43:22.970 --> 00:43:24.833
I have said things that are wrong.

00:43:24.833 --> 00:43:26.650
I'll tell you something I was wrong about.

00:43:26.650 --> 00:43:28.110
And it's this next one.

00:43:28.110 --> 00:43:28.943
Okay.

00:43:28.943 --> 00:43:30.780
Just to get, just to
lighten it up a little bit.

00:43:30.780 --> 00:43:31.613
Rachel said,

00:43:31.613 --> 00:43:36.227
"I insisted we would never
have pets inside the house.

00:43:36.227 --> 00:43:38.277
"Then we got our cat and I insisted

00:43:38.277 --> 00:43:39.727
"she'd never be allowed in the bedroom,

00:43:39.727 --> 00:43:41.857
"but now both our cats sleep in the bed

00:43:41.857 --> 00:43:45.910
"and I would kill a man if
either of them requested it."

00:43:45.910 --> 00:43:48.500
Oh, wow, kill a man if
the cats requested it,

00:43:48.500 --> 00:43:50.780
talk about a reframing of allegiance.

00:43:50.780 --> 00:43:53.607
But I mean, yeah, for
years I was like, you know,

00:43:53.607 --> 00:43:57.887
"I am not gonna have pets in the house.

00:43:57.887 --> 00:44:00.600
"I am not, you know,
that's not who we are."

00:44:00.600 --> 00:44:02.560
And then I came up with other reasons

00:44:02.560 --> 00:44:04.930
to tell the kids why
it wasn't gonna happen.

00:44:04.930 --> 00:44:08.330
But like, I just, you know, I
didn't like the idea of hair.

00:44:08.330 --> 00:44:11.660
I didn't like the idea of the poop,

00:44:11.660 --> 00:44:14.180
even if the poop was outside, I mean ...

00:44:14.180 --> 00:44:15.680
You don't even want poop on the perimeter.

00:44:15.680 --> 00:44:19.220
Every single time I'm just, you know,

00:44:19.220 --> 00:44:22.350
doting over Jade or babying her.

00:44:22.350 --> 00:44:24.250
You know, the kids still
love to point out like,

00:44:24.250 --> 00:44:27.360
"Aren't you glad that you
were wrong about that?"

00:44:27.360 --> 00:44:31.310
It's like, you know what,
and it's an opportunity

00:44:31.310 --> 00:44:32.834
for me to say, "You know what?

00:44:32.834 --> 00:44:37.347
"Kids, I'm glad that
you forced this issue.

00:44:37.347 --> 00:44:40.830
"Lily, in particular, with the dog."

00:44:40.830 --> 00:44:43.470
I'm gonna save any
conversation about the cat

00:44:43.470 --> 00:44:48.470
that does live in our home
for a subsequent thing.

00:44:48.610 --> 00:44:49.806
Yeah.

00:44:49.806 --> 00:44:51.120
I will acknowledge that there's,

00:44:52.286 --> 00:44:53.460
we now have a dog and a cat animal

00:44:53.460 --> 00:44:55.230
living inside of our house.

00:44:55.230 --> 00:44:56.063
Mm-hmm.

00:44:57.000 --> 00:44:58.230
But I try to acknowledge.

00:44:58.230 --> 00:44:59.557
Yeah, I take it as a moment to be like,

00:44:59.557 --> 00:45:03.950
"Yeah, I am grateful that I
have done a complete 180."

00:45:03.950 --> 00:45:05.500
Like my life is so enriched

00:45:05.500 --> 00:45:07.440
and then I'll just start
gushing about Jade.

00:45:07.440 --> 00:45:11.230
But really I see it as an opportunity to,

00:45:11.230 --> 00:45:15.878
in an innocuous way, on an
innocuous topic, to say,

00:45:15.878 --> 00:45:18.830
"You know what?
"I changed my mind."

00:45:18.830 --> 00:45:20.800
I did change my perspective.

00:45:20.800 --> 00:45:23.270
It wasn't about being
right or being wrong.

00:45:23.270 --> 00:45:28.270
It's not about morality here,
but it's about humility.

00:45:28.880 --> 00:45:30.567
Of saying, "You know what?

00:45:30.567 --> 00:45:34.557
"I did change my mind and I
disagree with my former self.

00:45:34.557 --> 00:45:39.557
"And my life is better because I changed

00:45:41.450 --> 00:45:42.860
"than it was before."

00:45:42.860 --> 00:45:44.310
And don't you think that's
really the heart of it?

00:45:44.310 --> 00:45:47.700
Is the fact that when
you didn't have animals

00:45:47.700 --> 00:45:50.720
in the house, the only
thing that you had to go on

00:45:50.720 --> 00:45:53.340
was the perceived negative aspects

00:45:53.340 --> 00:45:54.750
of having animals in the house, right?

00:45:54.750 --> 00:45:57.180
It's like you can't
really perceive positives

00:45:57.180 --> 00:45:58.800
without experiencing them,

00:45:58.800 --> 00:46:02.100
but you can kind of anticipate negatives.

00:46:02.100 --> 00:46:03.690
It's much easier, I don't know why,

00:46:03.690 --> 00:46:05.230
it's just the way our brains work.

00:46:05.230 --> 00:46:07.361
But then when you began to experience

00:46:07.361 --> 00:46:09.840
the love that you can have for a dog,

00:46:09.840 --> 00:46:11.824
which started, you know?

00:46:11.824 --> 00:46:14.510
And a love that wasn't the
same kind of love that like

00:46:14.510 --> 00:46:16.120
we had for our dogs growing up,

00:46:16.120 --> 00:46:18.150
that like were like out in the shed

00:46:18.150 --> 00:46:19.363
in the back kind of thing, you know?

00:46:19.363 --> 00:46:21.820
They were more like, we
had just had like a wolf

00:46:21.820 --> 00:46:24.580
that we happened to round up.

00:46:24.580 --> 00:46:26.790
No, an inside the house dog that like

00:46:26.790 --> 00:46:29.770
you receive this physical
love from on a regular basis.

00:46:29.770 --> 00:46:34.140
Like your standards will
begin to change very quickly,

00:46:34.140 --> 00:46:36.340
because you see the other
side of the coin, right?

00:46:36.340 --> 00:46:37.173
And then you're like,

00:46:37.173 --> 00:46:38.850
"Oh. there are going to
be some inconveniences."

00:46:38.850 --> 00:46:40.400
I mean, one of the things
that I think about,

00:46:40.400 --> 00:46:42.032
and we've talked about this is

00:46:42.032 --> 00:46:45.680
when you decide to love something

00:46:46.520 --> 00:46:48.112
then you start thinking
about the fact that

00:46:48.112 --> 00:46:52.460
this thing is not, this thing
doesn't live as long as me.

00:46:52.460 --> 00:46:56.810
Like this dog will die most
likely before I will, right?

00:46:56.810 --> 00:46:59.505
If we both live to our
general life expectancy,

00:46:59.505 --> 00:47:01.360
I'm gonna outlive Barbara.

00:47:01.360 --> 00:47:02.657
And now I think about,

00:47:02.657 --> 00:47:05.510
"Dang, that's gonna be really difficult."

00:47:05.510 --> 00:47:07.240
But I'm still willing
to make the sacrifice,

00:47:07.240 --> 00:47:11.040
because of the positive that
I'm experiencing right now.

00:47:11.040 --> 00:47:14.500
It's really about, and too,
if you try to take that

00:47:14.500 --> 00:47:19.237
and map it onto something that
is more moral, or, you know,

00:47:20.990 --> 00:47:23.080
I don't know exactly know how you do that.

00:47:23.080 --> 00:47:24.400
Like, how do you get someone to,

00:47:24.400 --> 00:47:25.600
I think it goes back to what you said,

00:47:25.600 --> 00:47:29.540
which is you got to make the
experience personal, right?

00:47:29.540 --> 00:47:30.930
It's not something that you're just gonna,

00:47:30.930 --> 00:47:35.240
you can't just sit and think
about the idea of a dog

00:47:35.240 --> 00:47:39.040
in your house in your head
and come to some conclusion

00:47:39.040 --> 00:47:41.330
to be like, "I love dogs in my house."

00:47:41.330 --> 00:47:43.990
It's like, you got to have a
dog in the house, you know?

00:47:43.990 --> 00:47:46.770
You gotta have a friend who
challenges your perspective.

00:47:46.770 --> 00:47:49.370
You gotta see something in somebody.

00:47:49.370 --> 00:47:51.280
You have to have a personal
experience in order

00:47:51.280 --> 00:47:54.080
to change your mind about
something ultimately, right?

00:47:54.080 --> 00:47:55.700
In your personal experiences it's with

00:47:55.700 --> 00:47:58.430
having a dog in the house
and now a cat in the house.

00:47:58.430 --> 00:48:02.580
And I'd much rather convey
that principle to my kids

00:48:02.580 --> 00:48:07.303
than having all the
answers or being right.

00:48:08.690 --> 00:48:12.096
It's being willing to be,
to admit when you're wrong

00:48:12.096 --> 00:48:14.790
or when you've changed
your mind on something

00:48:14.790 --> 00:48:16.570
that's not about right or wrong even.

00:48:16.570 --> 00:48:17.403
Yeah.

00:48:18.264 --> 00:48:21.050
It's more important than being right

00:48:21.050 --> 00:48:23.420
at any particular moment, you know?

00:48:23.420 --> 00:48:25.710
Because think about how miserable you'd be

00:48:25.710 --> 00:48:27.574
if the only thing you
were doing was focusing on

00:48:27.574 --> 00:48:31.550
continuing to bolster your
predetermined argument

00:48:31.550 --> 00:48:32.907
that having animals in the house was bad.

00:48:32.907 --> 00:48:35.875
I can imagine that there
are dads out there,

00:48:35.875 --> 00:48:38.371
moms, brothers, sisters,

00:48:38.371 --> 00:48:43.371
who I was dead set against
having a dog in the house,

00:48:43.710 --> 00:48:45.280
and now that there's a dog in the house

00:48:45.280 --> 00:48:47.060
I'm the person who doesn't like it

00:48:47.060 --> 00:48:48.690
and I spend all my time pointing out

00:48:48.690 --> 00:48:51.640
all the things that the
dog is doing that are bad.

00:48:51.640 --> 00:48:54.934
Like what a miserable
existence versus accepting it

00:48:54.934 --> 00:48:56.539
and staying positive.

00:48:56.539 --> 00:48:58.652
Yeah, because then you're
like secretly petting the dog.

00:48:58.652 --> 00:49:01.257
You don't want to be seen, right?

00:49:01.257 --> 00:49:05.210
You have a secret relationship,
and then that gets weird.

00:49:05.210 --> 00:49:06.660
You know?
Right.

00:49:06.660 --> 00:49:08.170
Secret petting.

00:49:08.170 --> 00:49:11.490
Well, you know, there's other ones,

00:49:11.490 --> 00:49:13.910
but we're not gonna get to
them for the sake of time,

00:49:13.910 --> 00:49:15.507
because ...
We'll do this again.

00:49:15.507 --> 00:49:19.376
But the fact that we did
have such great responses

00:49:19.376 --> 00:49:22.670
and we still want to continue

00:49:22.670 --> 00:49:24.850
to encourage people to respond.

00:49:24.850 --> 00:49:27.437
And you know, one of the things
that we were talking about

00:49:27.437 --> 00:49:28.350
that I want to talk to them about,

00:49:28.350 --> 00:49:30.500
I want to talk to you about is

00:49:30.500 --> 00:49:35.289
if you've never responded
to a prompt on Ear Biscuits,

00:49:35.289 --> 00:49:37.386
maybe that's because you
don't follow us on Twitter

00:49:37.386 --> 00:49:42.386
or you're not the type
of person to respond

00:49:42.480 --> 00:49:45.759
to just open ended questions
associated with this show.

00:49:45.759 --> 00:49:47.266
On the internet.

00:49:47.266 --> 00:49:49.039
Or on the internet in general.

00:49:49.039 --> 00:49:51.180
But we want to invite you
to take a risk and do that.

00:49:51.180 --> 00:49:56.180
So if you follow our
@Mythical Twitter account,

00:49:56.870 --> 00:49:57.970
or our personal accounts,

00:49:57.970 --> 00:49:59.630
we can try to do a better job of

00:49:59.630 --> 00:50:02.090
re-tweeting those prompts as well.

00:50:02.090 --> 00:50:05.267
But definitely if you
follow the @Mythical account

00:50:05.267 --> 00:50:07.810
you can see every so often

00:50:07.810 --> 00:50:10.547
when we post these prompts
like this one about,

00:50:10.547 --> 00:50:12.160
"Tell us about when
you changed your mind."

00:50:12.160 --> 00:50:14.210
We just want to hear from more of you.

00:50:14.210 --> 00:50:17.190
We enjoy the familiar faces
and the familiar names

00:50:17.190 --> 00:50:20.050
and you really get that
sense of community.

00:50:20.050 --> 00:50:23.010
And so we're not complaining about that,

00:50:23.010 --> 00:50:26.170
but we do think there's a
whole group of people who ...

00:50:26.170 --> 00:50:27.003
Just kind of watching.

00:50:27.003 --> 00:50:29.815
You know, I bet you're a thoughtful lurker

00:50:29.815 --> 00:50:32.490
and we want to hear from
you on these topics.

00:50:32.490 --> 00:50:34.170
And sometimes the questions are tough.

00:50:34.170 --> 00:50:37.050
It might take a little
time to think about it,

00:50:37.050 --> 00:50:38.660
but you don't have to answer immediately.

00:50:38.660 --> 00:50:42.158
You can come back and we
usually give, you know,

00:50:42.158 --> 00:50:46.180
at least a day where you
can come back and add to it

00:50:46.180 --> 00:50:48.110
before we roll everything up.

00:50:48.110 --> 00:50:49.110
Yeah, I mean, like Link said,

00:50:49.110 --> 00:50:51.750
we definitely appreciate the folks

00:50:51.750 --> 00:50:53.507
who always respond to the prompts,

00:50:53.507 --> 00:50:56.603
but we are, you know, really interested

00:50:56.603 --> 00:50:59.730
in hearing from people
who've never responded,

00:50:59.730 --> 00:51:00.563
who might say,

00:51:00.563 --> 00:51:03.090
"I created a Twitter account
just so I could respond."

00:51:03.090 --> 00:51:04.943
So #EarBiscuits.

00:51:06.870 --> 00:51:09.230
You can continue to talk about
what we talked about today,

00:51:09.230 --> 00:51:10.990
but also be looking for that next prompt

00:51:10.990 --> 00:51:14.270
that we're gonna put out
there to get your questions.

00:51:14.270 --> 00:51:16.520
And now I'm going to give my rec,

00:51:16.520 --> 00:51:17.673
'cause it's my week.

00:51:19.640 --> 00:51:23.610
On Disney Plus, there's, you know,

00:51:23.610 --> 00:51:27.110
you can search by all the
different brands that they own

00:51:27.110 --> 00:51:31.220
like Marvel and Star Wars,
and it goes on from there.

00:51:31.220 --> 00:51:34.240
And, you know, they're
doing a lot of things

00:51:34.240 --> 00:51:38.390
where they're creating
content around content,

00:51:38.390 --> 00:51:41.620
like behind the scenes series
for the Mandalorian series,

00:51:41.620 --> 00:51:43.987
which is absolutely amazing.

00:51:43.987 --> 00:51:46.900
So I got curious, and
I was looking around.

00:51:46.900 --> 00:51:51.560
There's lots of that in Marvel,
the Marvel section as well,

00:51:51.560 --> 00:51:56.517
but there's one show called Marvel 616.

00:51:56.517 --> 00:51:59.450
And it's basically this concept of taking

00:52:00.510 --> 00:52:02.510
principles around the Marvel properties

00:52:02.510 --> 00:52:06.090
and just exploring these
like tertiary ideas

00:52:06.090 --> 00:52:07.730
or behind the scenes type things.

00:52:07.730 --> 00:52:10.080
They're all, each episode
is completely different,

00:52:10.080 --> 00:52:11.220
and I've only watched one.

00:52:11.220 --> 00:52:15.270
So I'm only recommending episode one,

00:52:15.270 --> 00:52:18.402
which is about the Japanese Spider-Man.

00:52:18.402 --> 00:52:19.446
Hm.

00:52:19.446 --> 00:52:21.110
Now I have, I've had a tee shirt for years

00:52:21.110 --> 00:52:22.010
that had Spider-Man,

00:52:22.010 --> 00:52:24.200
and then it had Japanese
writing underneath it.

00:52:24.200 --> 00:52:26.870
And I just thought it
was a cool tee shirt.

00:52:26.870 --> 00:52:30.073
Well, it turns out there's,

00:52:31.950 --> 00:52:36.950
Marvel licensed Spider-Man
over in Japan, too.

00:52:38.050 --> 00:52:41.700
And then they created a
television show for Spider-Man

00:52:41.700 --> 00:52:44.790
and it was entirely different.

00:52:44.790 --> 00:52:47.200
It was, you know,

00:52:47.200 --> 00:52:49.840
there was some emissary
who pitched it to Marvel.

00:52:49.840 --> 00:52:51.810
And then, it's a documentary on

00:52:51.810 --> 00:52:54.820
creating an alternate universe,

00:52:54.820 --> 00:52:57.290
basically a Japanese version of Spider-Man

00:52:57.290 --> 00:52:58.663
that is totally different.

00:52:58.663 --> 00:52:59.850
Does he have a suit?

00:52:59.850 --> 00:53:02.540
He looks the same, it's the same suit,

00:53:02.540 --> 00:53:06.110
but he's, the action
adventure that he's in,

00:53:06.110 --> 00:53:08.890
like it's not Peter Parker.

00:53:08.890 --> 00:53:11.150
Well, that makes sense.
Right.

00:53:11.150 --> 00:53:12.610
And he's not in New York,

00:53:12.610 --> 00:53:17.010
but I mean, like he fights
robots, he like drives vehicles.

00:53:17.010 --> 00:53:19.530
He still behaves like a spider,

00:53:19.530 --> 00:53:23.780
and they talk about like
how they made the show.

00:53:23.780 --> 00:53:26.170
And it's just absolutely fascinating.

00:53:26.170 --> 00:53:27.291
Is this a one-off documentary or?

00:53:27.291 --> 00:53:30.313
It's a one-off, just that
one, the one episode.

00:53:32.790 --> 00:53:33.670
And, you know,

00:53:33.670 --> 00:53:37.660
it's all of the people
who were involved in it.

00:53:37.660 --> 00:53:39.656
They even talked to the
stuntman who were like,

00:53:39.656 --> 00:53:44.656
there's this iconic tower in Tokyo.

00:53:45.090 --> 00:53:47.540
I think it's called the Tokyo Tower.

00:53:47.540 --> 00:53:49.040
I'm probably getting
all this wrong, but ...

00:53:49.040 --> 00:53:50.320
Well, that's a good guess.

00:53:50.320 --> 00:53:53.410
Well, I mean, they tell the
story of him showing up,

00:53:53.410 --> 00:53:55.610
of the stunt man showing
up on one of his first days

00:53:55.610 --> 00:53:57.300
and he puts on the Spider-Man costume

00:53:57.300 --> 00:53:58.660
and then they just tell him, "Okay."

00:53:58.660 --> 00:54:00.580
He's just showing up for
work to be Spider-Man

00:54:00.580 --> 00:54:03.110
and they're like, "Climb the tower."

00:54:03.110 --> 00:54:06.280
With no net, no safety mechanisms.

00:54:06.280 --> 00:54:09.610
He free climbs this tower
and then they use that shot

00:54:09.610 --> 00:54:12.200
in like the closing or
opening credits of the show.

00:54:12.200 --> 00:54:13.570
I mean, you want to get your money's worth

00:54:13.570 --> 00:54:15.500
if someone's gonna put
their life on the line.

00:54:15.500 --> 00:54:17.657
But all the stuff this guy
would do, like they would,

00:54:17.657 --> 00:54:19.424
they didn't have any
budget, but they would,

00:54:19.424 --> 00:54:23.730
it was important that they
were like high-action.

00:54:23.730 --> 00:54:24.993
What years are we talking about?

00:54:24.993 --> 00:54:26.230
This is the '70s.
Okay.

00:54:26.230 --> 00:54:28.810
And like, so he would fight
these gigantic robots,

00:54:28.810 --> 00:54:33.810
because you had to have gigantic
robots in Japanese shows.

00:54:34.545 --> 00:54:35.378
Right.

00:54:35.378 --> 00:54:36.529
That's what all of them were.

00:54:36.529 --> 00:54:37.362
Right.

00:54:37.362 --> 00:54:41.140
So it's at every turn
in everything they do,

00:54:41.140 --> 00:54:45.810
it's just splendidly
strange to us as Americans

00:54:45.810 --> 00:54:47.230
who know our Spider-Man,

00:54:47.230 --> 00:54:50.347
but because, you know, they
were able to separate things

00:54:50.347 --> 00:54:51.700
and there was no internet.

00:54:51.700 --> 00:54:53.770
And like, they didn't share shows.

00:54:53.770 --> 00:54:54.957
And Marvel said,

00:54:54.957 --> 00:54:56.257
"Do whatever you want with Spider-Man

00:54:56.257 --> 00:54:58.427
"to make it work over here in Japan,

00:54:58.427 --> 00:55:00.580
"but it can't leave Japan."

00:55:00.580 --> 00:55:01.847
Right, and it could be
contained at the time.

00:55:01.847 --> 00:55:03.920
And it could be contained.

00:55:03.920 --> 00:55:06.770
And so now if you watch these clips,

00:55:06.770 --> 00:55:08.980
it's just like bonkers to us

00:55:08.980 --> 00:55:11.570
and the stories are equally bonkers.

00:55:11.570 --> 00:55:13.450
So I would definitely recommend that,

00:55:13.450 --> 00:55:15.960
Marvel 616 episode one.

00:55:15.960 --> 00:55:18.670
Episode one, maybe
you'll watch episode two.

